─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THEORY 1 FORMULATED: LID SWITCH / LOGIND SUSPEND STORM
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Response at the time was overly confident:
"Excellent find — this is your smoking gun."

Reasoning:
- systemd-logind has HandleLidSwitch=suspend default
- Kernel lockdown blocks suspend on secure-boot systems
- Retry loop causes burst of suspend attempts
- Each attempt: dbus call, PolicyKit check, log writes
- Pattern matching: bursts correlate with IO spikes

Supporting table built:
| Time | Count | Note |
|------|-------|------|
| 23:01:31 | 2 | Normal 30-min heartbeat |
| 23:31:31 | 2 | Normal |
| 23:37:27 | 8 | ⚠️ Burst |
| 01:01:31 | 2 | Normal |
| 01:31:31 | 2 | Normal |
| 01:43:38 | 10 | ⚠️ Matches IO spike at 01:41 |
| 01:45:15 | 2+ | "callbacks suppressed" |

"callbacks suppressed" interpreted as rate-limit, meaning real count
was higher than 10.

